  "entries": [
    {
      "id": "OP-SEED",
      "kind": "operation",
      "operation": "seed",
      "source": "itc2008",
      "cite": "269#6e3-270#68d",
      "statement": "ITC uses the seed stamp (1, 0) as the initial state from which forks are made to obtain an initial configuration."
    },
    {
      "id": "OP-FORK",
      "kind": "operation",
      "operation": "fork",
      "source": "itc2008",
      "cite": "82#be4-87#ab2",
      "statement": "fork takes a stamp (i, e) and returns two stamps ((i1, e), (i2, e)) such that i2 != i1, i1 + i2 = i, and i1 * i2 = 0. The event component is preserved and the identity is split into two non-overlapping parts."
    },
    {
      "id": "OP-EVENT",
      "kind": "operation",
      "operation": "event",
      "source": "itc2008",
      "cite": "94#d59-99#69a",
      "statement": "event takes a stamp (i, e) and returns (i, e0) such that e < e0 in the causal order. In version vectors, event increments the counter associated to the identity: e0[k] = e[k] for k != i, and e0[i] = e[i] + 1. Event has the precondition that i != 0 (non-null identity)."
    },
    {
      "id": "OP-JOIN",
      "kind": "operation",
      "operation": "join",
      "source": "itc2008",
      "cite": "101#6a0-108#69e",
      "statement": "join takes two stamps ((i1, e1), (i2, e2)) and returns (i3, e3) where e3 = e1 ⊔ e2 (the join semilattice join), i3 = f(i1, i2). The resulting event component must dominate both e1 and e2, and must not dominate more than either did."
    },
    {
      "id": "OP-PEEK",
      "kind": "operation",
      "operation": "peek",
      "source": "itc2008",
      "cite": "89#3ec-92#7ef",
      "statement": "peek is a special case of fork: peek((i, e)) = ((0, e), (i, e)). It returns an anonymous stamp (0, e) with null identity and a copy with the original identity."
    },
    {
      "id": "OP-SEND",
      "kind": "operation",
      "operation": "send",
      "source": "itc2008",
      "cite": "119#6b9-120#193",
      "statement": "send is the atomic composition of event followed by peek. In vector clock systems, message sending is modeled by incrementing the local counter and then creating a new message."
    },
    {
      "id": "OP-RECEIVE",
      "kind": "operation",
      "operation": "receive",
      "source": "itc2008",
      "cite": "122#820-123#170",
      "statement": "receive is the atomic composition of join followed by event. In vector clocks, taking the pointwise maximum is followed by an increment of the local counter."
    },
    {
      "id": "OP-SYNC",
      "kind": "operation",
      "operation": "sync",
      "source": "itc2008",
      "cite": "125#3f9-126#a1b",
      "statement": "sync is the atomic composition of join followed by fork. In version vector systems and bounded version vectors, it models the atomic synchronization of two replicas."
    },
    {
      "id": "ID-TREE-RECURSIVE",
      "kind": "operation",
      "operation": "id tree structure",
      "source": "itc2008",
      "cite": "228#524-230#4d8",
      "statement": "The id component is an id tree with recursive form: i ::= 0 | 1 | (i1, i2) where i, i1, i2 are id trees."
    },
    {
      "id": "ID-TREE-SEMANTICS",
      "kind": "operation",
      "operation": "id tree semantics",
      "source": "itc2008",
      "cite": "232#5b2-236#de0",
      "statement": "Semantic interpretation of id trees as functions: J0K = 0, J1K = 1, J(i1, i2)K = λx. Ji1K(2x) + Ji2K(2x − 1). For (i1, i2), i1 maps to [0, 1/2) and i2 maps to [1/2, 1)."
    },
    {
      "id": "EVENT-TREE-RECURSIVE",
      "kind": "operation",
      "operation": "event tree structure",
      "source": "itc2008",
      "cite": "248#2c3-250#98e",
      "statement": "The event component is a binary event tree: e ::= n | (n, e1, e2) where e, e1, e2 are event trees and n is a non-negative integer."
    },
    {
      "id": "EVENT-TREE-SEMANTICS",
      "kind": "operation",
      "operation": "event tree semantics",
      "source": "itc2008",
      "cite": "252#7e5-255#dda",
      "statement": "Semantic interpretation of event trees: JnK = n · 1, J(n, e1, e2)K = n · 1 + λx. Je1K(2x) + Je2K(2x − 1)."
    },
    {
      "id": "ID-NORM",
      "kind": "operation",
      "operation": "id tree normalization",
      "source": "itc2008",
      "cite": "295#ce4-300#2ed",
      "statement": "Id tree normalization: norm((0, 0)) = 0, norm((1, 1)) = 1, norm(i) = i otherwise."
    },
    {
      "id": "EVENT-NORM",
      "kind": "operation",
      "operation": "event tree normalization",
      "source": "itc2008",
      "cite": "314#509-319#4ca",
      "statement": "Event tree normalization: norm(n) = n; norm((n, m, m)) = n + m; norm((n, e1, e2)) = (n + m, e1↓m, e2↓m) where m = min(min(e1), min(e2))."
    },
    {
      "id": "SPLIT-FUNC",
      "kind": "operation",
      "operation": "split function",
      "source": "itc2008",
      "cite": "363#6e5-370#48c",
      "statement": "Split function for fork: split(0) = (0, 0); split(1) = ((1, 0), (0, 1)); split((0, i)) = ((0, i1), (0, i2)) where (i1, i2) = split(i); split((i, 0)) = ((i1, 0), (i2, 0)) where (i1, i2) = split(i); split((i1, i2)) = ((i1, 0), (0, i2))."
    },
    {
      "id": "SUM-FUNC",
      "kind": "operation",
      "operation": "sum function (id)",
      "source": "itc2008",
      "cite": "384#475-388#e7c",
      "statement": "Sum function for join: sum(0, i) = i; sum(i, 0) = i; sum((l1, r1), (l2, r2)) = norm((sum(l1, l2), sum(r1, r2)))."
    },
    {
      "id": "EVENT-JOIN-FUNC",
      "kind": "operation",
      "operation": "event join function",
      "source": "itc2008",
      "cite": "390#dae-396#4f9",
      "statement": "Event tree join: join(n1, n2) = max(n1, n2); join(n1, (n2, l2, r2)) = join((n1, 0, 0), (n2, l2, r2)); join((n1, l1, r1), n2) = join((n1, l1, r1), (n2, 0, 0)); join((n1, l1, r1), (n2, l2, r2)) = join((n2, l2, r2), (n1, l1, r1)) if n1 > n2; join((n1, l1, r1), (n2, l2, r2)) = norm((n1, join(l1, l2↑(n2−n1)), join(r1, r2↑(n2−n1))))."
    },
    {
      "id": "COMPARE-FUNC",
      "kind": "operation",
      "operation": "comparison function",
      "source": "itc2008",
      "cite": "345#d07-355#17e",
      "statement": "Comparison: (i1, e1) ≤ (i2, e2) iff leq(e1, e2). leq(n1, n2) = n1 ≤ n2; leq(n1, (n2, l2, r2)) = n1 ≤ n2; leq((n1, l1, r1), n2) = n1 ≤ n2 ∧ leq(l1↑n1, n2) ∧ leq(r1↑n1, n2); leq((n1, l1, r1), (n2, l2, r2)) = n1 ≤ n2 ∧ leq(l1↑n1, l2↑n2) ∧ leq(r1↑n1, r2↑n2)."
    },
    {
      "id": "FILL-FUNC",
      "kind": "operation",
      "operation": "fill function (event)",
      "source": "itc2008",
      "cite": "426#7f5-434#5fe",
      "statement": "Fill function: fill(0, e) = e; fill(1, e) = max(e); fill(i, n) = n; fill((1, ir), (n, el, er)) = norm((n, max(max(el), min(e0r)), e0r)) where e0r = fill(ir, er); fill((il, 1), (n, el, er)) = norm((n, e0l, max(max(er), min(e0l)))) where e0l = fill(il, el); fill((il, ir), (n, el, er)) = norm((n, fill(il, el), fill(ir, er)))."
    },
    {
      "id": "GROW-FUNC",
      "kind": "operation",
      "operation": "grow function (event)",
      "source": "itc2008",
      "cite": "453#7e0-462#955",
      "statement": "Grow function: grow(1, n) = (n + 1, 0); grow(i, n) = (e0, c + N) where (e0, c) = grow(i, (n, 0, 0)) and N is a large constant; grow((0, ir), (n, el, er)) = ((n, el, e0r), cr + 1) where (e0r, cr) = grow(ir, er); grow((il, 0), (n, el, er)) = ((n, e0l, er), cl + 1) where (e0l, cl) = grow(il, el); grow((il, ir), (n, el, er)) = ((n, e0l, er), cl + 1) if cl < cr, or ((n, el, e0r), cr + 1) if cl >= cr."
    },
    {
      "id": "EVENT-FULL",
      "kind": "operation",
      "operation": "event full definition",
      "source": "itc2008",
      "cite": "417#32e-420#02d",
      "statement": "Event: event((i, e)) = (i, fill(i, e)) if fill(i, e) ≠ e, otherwise (i, e0) where (e0, c) = grow(i, e). Subject to: Je0K = JeK + f · JiK for any f such that f · JiK ≠ 0. Event cannot be applied to anonymous stamps (i = 0)."
    },
    {
      "id": "EXPLODE-OP",
      "kind": "operation",
      "operation": "explode",
      "source": "interval-tree-clocks",
      "cite": "118#0cf-119#e3b",
      "statement": "Explode splits a single ID+event counter part of a clock into independent components (local ID and event counter separately). Used to avoid rewriting all records on fork."
    },
    {
      "id": "REBUILD-OP",
      "kind": "operation",
      "operation": "rebuild",
      "source": "interval-tree-clocks",
      "cite": "118#0cf-119#e3b",
      "statement": "Rebuild reassembles the ID and event counter parts of a clock. At each operation, the node's ID is submitted on each operation and the event counter on the rest."
    },
    {
      "id": "RECEIVE-ITC-OP",
      "kind": "operation",
      "operation": "receive (ITC variant)",
      "source": "interval-tree-clocks",
      "cite": "190#bd4-198#c4b",
      "statement": "ITC receive variant: explode to get only the event counter, event to increment, join to merge, peek to complete the remote stamp as read-only, then rebuild to make a complete local stamp."
    },
    {
      "id": "RECEIVE-VC-OP",
      "kind": "operation",
      "operation": "receive vector clock variant",
      "source": "interval-tree-clocks",
      "cite": "212#394-218#26e",
      "statement": "Vector clock equivalence receive: fetch current stamp; if current >= received, merge both counters and increment with local id; if current < received, store new data with merged counters incremented with local id."
    },
    {
      "id": "SYNC-ITC-OP",
      "kind": "operation",
      "operation": "sync (ITC variant)",
      "source": "interval-tree-clocks",
      "cite": "58#7ed-59#e3b",
      "statement": "sync in ITC: leave the IDs as they are, but merge the event counter. Atomic composition of join + fork."
    },
